{"study_accession":"PRJNA929482","secondary_study_accession":"SRP420036","title":"First-in-Human Study in Healthy Subjects with the Non-Cytotoxic Monoclonal Antibody OSE-127, a Strict Antagonist of the IL-7Rα","description":"OSE-127 is a humanized monoclonal antibody targeting the IL-7Rα chain (CD127), under development for inflammatory and autoimmune disease treatment. It is a strict antagonist of the IL-7R pathway, is not internalized by target cells and is non-cytotoxic. Here, a first-in-human, phase I, randomized, double-blind, placebo-controlled, single-center study was carried out to determine the safety, pharmacokinetics, pharmacodynamics, and immunogenicity of OSE-127 administration.Sixty-three healthy subjects were randomly assigned to nine groups: six single ascending dose groups with intravenous (IV) administration (0.002-10 mg/kg), a single subcutaneous treatment group (1 mg/kg), and two double IV injection groups (6 or 10 mg/kg). Subjects were followed during < 146 days. OSE-127’s pharmacokinetic half-life after a single dose increased from 4.6 days (1mg/kg) to 11.7 days (10 mg/kg) and, after a second dose, from 12.5 days (6 mg/kg) to 16.25 days (10 mg/kg). Receptor occupancy was ≥ 95% at doses ≥ 0.02 mg/kg and this saturation level was maintained >100 days after two IV infusions at 10 mg/kg. IL-7 consumption was inhibited by OSE-127 administration, as demonstrated by a decreased IL-7 pathway gene signature in peripheral blood cells and by ex-vivo T-lymphocyte restimulation experiments. OSE-127 was well tolerated, with no evidence of cytokine-release syndrome and no significant alteration of blood lymphocyte counts or subset populations.Altogether, the observed lack of significant lymphopenia or serious adverse events, concomitant with the dose-dependent inhibition of IL-7 consumption by target cells highlight that OSE-127 may show clinical activity in IL-7R pathway-involved diseases. Overall design: 22 paired samples (at baseline, Day15, Day57) were collected on Day 15 after administration, and 4 paired samples were collected on Day 57 for transcriptomic analysis to follow the antiIL7R signature on Healthy volunteers.","pubmed":"36734626","geo_series":"","n_samples":46,"study_labels":[],"samples":[{"sample_accession":"SAMN32960251","study_accession":"PRJNA929482","blood_class":"whole_blood","class_from":"sample","title":"29007_S154_priod2","description":"29007_S154_priod2","sex":"","geo_sample":"GSM7010279","access":"open","dbgap":"","n_runs":1,"first_public":"2023-02-02","attributes":[["source_name","blood"],["tissue","blood"],["time","D15"],["treatment","OSE127"],["dose","10 mg/kg"]],"runs":[{"run_accession":"SRR23270914","experiment_accession":"SRX19214669","library_layout":"PAIRED","library_selection":"cDNA","instrument_model":"Illumina HiSeq 2500","read_count":23137009,"base_count":6941102700,"first_public":"2023-02-02","experiment_title":"Illumina HiSeq 2500 sequencing: GSM7010279: 29007_S154_priod2 Homo sapiens RNA-Seq"}],"labels":[],"single_cell":false},{"sample_accession":"SAMN32960252","study_accession":"PRJNA929482","blood_class":"whole_blood","class_from":"sample","title":"29007_S154_priod1","description":"29007_S154_priod1","sex":"","geo_sample":"GSM7010278","access":"open","dbgap":"","n_runs":1,"first_public":"2023-02-02","attributes":[["source_name","blood"],["tissue","blood"],["time","D1"],["treatment","OSE127"],["dose","10 mg/kg"]],"runs":[{"run_accession":"SRR23270915","experiment_accession":"SRX19214668","library_layout":"PAIRED","library_selection":"cDNA","instrument_model":"Illumina HiSeq 2500","read_count":24597215,"base_count":7379164500,"first_public":"2023-02-02","experiment_title":"Illumina HiSeq 2500 sequencing: GSM7010278: 29007_S154_priod1 Homo sapiens RNA-Seq"}],"labels":[],"single_cell":false},{"sample_accession":"SAMN32960253","study_accession":"PRJNA929482","blood_class":"whole_blood","class_from":"sample","title":"29006_S149_priod2","description":"29006_S149_priod2","sex":"","geo